/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13954664/state.json" "file_sha256" "cbb323e2e80fffcb5b3d5b27ed767c90db63e4d5a7e12621e7c9826cf4a83901" "basis" 619 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13954664/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-11751" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}^{2}=8x"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13954664/state.json" "file_sha256" "cbb323e2e80fffcb5b3d5b27ed767c90db63e4d5a7e12621e7c9826cf4a83901" "basis" 619 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13954664/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-115640" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13954664/assets/c88e54f2a7acec3815a53fa4b0d641dd370ccf708b876f8dd231f05e115ea6fc.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13954664/state.json" "file_sha256" "cbb323e2e80fffcb5b3d5b27ed767c90db63e4d5a7e12621e7c9826cf4a83901" "basis" 619 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13954664/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-115640" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the right. The parabola has its vertex at $(3,3)$ and passes through the point $(6,-3).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(3,3),$ we substitute $h=3$ and $k=3$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-3)^{2} & =4p(x-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(6,-3),$ so we can substitute $x=6$ and $y=-3$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-3)^{2} & =4p(x-3) \\\\ (-3-3)^{2} & =4p(6-3) \\\\ 36 & =12p \\\\ p & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-3)^{2} & =4p(x-3) \\\\ (y-3)^{2} & =4(3)(x-3) \\\\ (y-3)^{2} & =12(x-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the right since $p=3$ is positive." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13954664/state.json" "file_sha256" "cbb323e2e80fffcb5b3d5b27ed767c90db63e4d5a7e12621e7c9826cf4a83901" "basis" 619 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13954664/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-115640" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13954664/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4db46a9d62f9a21d8f80a9f94b90e4fb4fe87c5b80b813f4fc9c8610a9418521" "basis" 619 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13954664/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-115640" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13954664/state.json" "file_sha256" "cbb323e2e80fffcb5b3d5b27ed767c90db63e4d5a7e12621e7c9826cf4a83901" "basis" 619 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13954664/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-115640" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "(x-3)^{2}=-12(y-3)"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Co