y/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13972835/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a7d27879639064186161f14cc9e606595f1bdeea14703939d4499f6ed286ec0" "basis" 666 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13972835/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-115648" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(0,2),$ we substitute $h=0$ and $k=2$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =4px.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(5,5),$ so we can substitute $x=5$ and $y=5$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =4px \\\\ (5-2)^{2} & =4p(5) \\\\ 9 & =20p \\\\ p & =\\frac{9}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =4px \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =4(\\frac{9}{20})x \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =\\frac{9}{5}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13972835/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a7d27879639064186161f14cc9e606595f1bdeea14703939d4499f6ed286ec0" "basis" 666 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13972835/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-115648" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13972835/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "25fc2a63ccb547609ccd88b96d2ba4646ed4dc26e2936e05de4d1f171bdd7563" "basis" 666 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13972835/edb-import/verification.j