=1$ and $y=2$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+3)^{2} & =4p(x+4) \\\\ (2+3)^{2} & =4p(1+4) \\\\ 25 & =20p \\\\ p & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+3)^{2} & =4p(x+4) \\\\ (y+3)^{2} & =4(\\frac{5}{4})(x+4) \\\\ (y+3)^{2} & =5(x+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041337/state.json" "file_sha256" "610aa6b2bb4ffb9544fff25cbc0cb9921548e03c007431fe4f7a0356ca138ec9"} {"question" "q-11812" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041337/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2b88b47ffba4f1cca609405a7a46f39d9237523db60eb50068f0e025412a3aa0"} {"question" "q-11812" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041337/state.json" "file_sha256" "610aa6b2bb4ffb9544fff25cbc0cb9921548e03c007431fe4f7a0356ca138ec9"} {"question" "q-11812" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(y+3)^{2}=-2(x+4)"] ["math" "(y+3)^{2}=-5(x+4)"] ["math" "(y+3)^{2}=2(x+4)"] ["math" "(y+3)^{2}=5(x+4)"] ["math" "(y+4)^{2}=5(x+3)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041337/state.json" "file_sha256" "610aa6b2bb4ffb9544fff25cbc0cb9921548e03c007431fe4f7a0356ca138ec9"} {"question" "q-11812" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(y+3)^{2}=5(x+4)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041337/state.json" "file_sha256" "610aa6b2bb4ffb9544fff25cbc0cb9921548e03c007431fe4f7a0356ca138ec9"} {"question" "e-5333" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041337/assets/70ff1c84aa4a9a2adc393fc1dcf684ee0bcf161671b15f9061e2319f5d7e0a39.png)\n\n  \n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041337/example-5333.json" "file_sha256" "797901f85674f0ab0d7f8dae78ebf5544683af628866459d19f19bfd3086fe99"} {"question" "e-5333" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the diagram, we see that the arms of the parabola open to the left. The parabola has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(-8,4).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\ny^2  = 4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(-8,4),$ we can substitute $x = -8$ and $y = 4$ into the standard equation and solve for $p\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {4}^{2} & =4p(-8) \\\\ 16 & =-32p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(-\\frac{1}{2})x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the left since $p = -\\dfrac{1}{2}$ is negative." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041337/example-5333.json" "file_sha256" "797901f85674f0ab0d7f8dae78ebf5544683af628866459d19f19bfd3086fe99"} {"question" "e-5334" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the equation of the right-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(4,4).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041337/example-5334.json" "file_sha256" "aa6f04a3653a822bb7622bbfa4ba4ebb234803fe381c2d2b2f2281d9bc967ec7"} {"question" "e-5334" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\ny^2  = 4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(4,4),$ we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=4$ into the standard equation and solve for $p\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {4}^{2} & =4p(4) \\\\ 16 & =16p \\\\ p & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(1)x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041337/example-5334.json" "file_sha256" "aa6f04a3653a822bb7622bbfa4ba4ebb234803fe381c2d2b2f2281d9bc967ec7"} {"question" "e-5335" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041337/assets/55651f756b31405e08cd96b097101528ffabff1e6411d3fcae9f40f747f607b4.png)\n\n  \n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041337/example-5335.json" "file_sha256" "fd885a4df879fad0b43d7bccfd1001da39aef7c941e0075d60d877075969f386"} {"question" "e-5335" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the left. The parabola has its vertex at $(4,2)$ and passes through the point $(0,6).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h, k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h, k) = (4,2),$ we substitute $h=4$ and $k=2$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =4p(x-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(0,6),$ so we can substitute $x=0$ and $y=6$ into the equation above and solve for $p\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =4p(x-4) \\\\ (6-2)^{2} & =4p(0-4) \\\\ 16 & =-16p \\\\ p & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =4p(x-4) \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =4(-1)(x-4) \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =-4(x-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the left since $p = -1$ is negative." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041337/example-5335.json" "file_sha256" "fd885a4df879fad0b43d7bccfd1001da39aef7c941e0075d60d877075969f386"} {"question" "e-5336" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the equation of the left-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(2,-2)$ and passes through the point $(-6,2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041337/example-5336.json" "file_sha256" "53bb73222334cecfbea4526233bdb94185612e1a1b751e3827b5ad1c9f9f5a42"} {"question" "e-5336" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h, k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h, k) = (2,-2),$ we substitute $h=2$ and $k=-2$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+2)^{2} & =4p(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(-6, 2),$ so we can substitute $x=-6$ and $y=2$ into the equation above and solve for $p\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+2)^{2} & =4p(x-2) \\\\ (2+2)^{2} & =4p(-6-2) \\\\ 16 & =-32p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+2)^{2} & =4p(x-2) \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =4(-\\frac{1}{2})(x-2) \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =-2(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041337/example-5336.json" "file_sha256" "53bb73222334cecfbea4526233bdb94185612e1a1b751e3827b5ad1c9f9f5a42"}] "usage" {"q-11807" [] "q-115633" [] "q-115640" [] "q-11796" [] "e-5333" [] "q-11755" [] "q-11751" [] "e-5334" [] "e-5336" [] "q-115648" [] "q-11794" [] "e-5335" []} "basis" 1498 "retractions" [[17592186096915 :question/problem "Find the equation of the left-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(0, 0)$ and passes through the point $(-12,-4)$."] [17592186275614 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13972835/assets/e4b681e9bdf5fa8021ca359e0bf93c27b53a18f0d5e906765a911a72ecfc0159.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?"] [17592186266687 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13954664/assets/c88e54f2a7acec3815a53fa4b0d641dd370ccf708b876f8dd231f05e115ea6fc.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?"] [17592186096925 :question/problem "Find the equation of the right-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(-1, 1)$ and passes through the point $(8, 7)$."] [17592186096423 :question/problem "What is the equation of the parabola shown above?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1124/Source/Images/d05aa50677877b505dd8f37cac612630.png)"] [17592186096423 :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that the arms of the parabola open to the left. The parabola has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(-8,4)$.\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n$y^2 = 4px$.\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(-8,4)$, we can substitute $x = -8$ and $y = 4$ into the standard equation and solve for $p$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{2} &= 4px \\\\\n4^{2} &= 4p(-8) \\\\\n16 &= -32p \\\\\np &= -\\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{2} &= 4px \\\\\ny^{2} &= 4(-\\frac{1}{2})x \\\\\ny^{2} &= -2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the left since\n\n$$\np = -\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nis negative."] [17592186096424 :question/problem "Find the equation of the right-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(4,4)$."] [17592186096424 :question/worked-solution "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n$y^2 = 4px$.\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(4,4)$, we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=4$ into the standard equation and solve for $p$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{2} &= 4px \\\\\n4^{2} &= 4p(4) \\\\\n16 &= 16p \\\\\np &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{2} &= 4px \\\\\ny^{2} &= 4(1)x \\\\\ny^{2} &= 4x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186096425 :question/problem "What is the equation of the parabola shown above?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1124/Source/Images/ba4f53d4bcb8d7c3ce8e264ba354d250.png)"] [17592186096425 :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the left. The parabola has its vertex at $(4,2)$ and passes through the point $(0,6)$.\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h, k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(y - k)^{2} &= 4p(x - h)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at\n\n$$\n(h, k) = (4,2)\n$$\n\nwe substitute $h=4$ and $k=2$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(y - 2)^{2} &= 4p(x - 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(0,6)$, so we can substitute $x=0$ and $y=6$ into the equation above and solve for $p$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(y - 2)^{2} &= 4p(x - 4) \\\\\n(6 - 2)^{2} &= 4p(0 - 4) \\\\\n16 &= -16p \\\\\np &= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(y - 2)^{2} &= 4p(x - 4) \\\\\n(y - 2)^{2} &= 4(-1)(x - 4) \\\\\n(y - 2)^{2} &= -4(x - 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the left since $p = -1$ is negative."] [17592186096426 :question/problem "Find the equation of the left-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(2,-2)$ and passes through the point $(-6,2)$."] [17592186096426 :question/worked-solution "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h, k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(y - k)^{2} &= 4p(x - h)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at\n\n$$\n(h, k) = (2,-2)\n$$\n\nwe substitute $h=2$ and $k=-2$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(y + 2)^{2} &= 4p(x - 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(-6, 2)$, so we can substitute $x=-6$ and $y=2$ into the equation above and solve for $p$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(y + 2)^{2} &= 4p(x - 2) \\\\\n(2 + 2)^{2} &= 4p(-6 - 2) \\\\\n16 &= -32p \\\\\np &= -\\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(y + 2)^{2} &= 4p(x - 2) \\\\\n(y + 2)^{2} &= 4(-\\frac{1}{2})(x - 2) \\\\\n(y + 2)^{2} &= -2(x - 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186093897 17592186093898 17592186093899 17592186093900 17592186093901 17592186093957 17592186093958 17592186093959 17592186093960 17592186093961 17592186266689 17592186266690 17592186266691 17592186266692 17592186266693 